//? Title: Fit an increasing rate with weighted steps
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Pool observations that would reverse the fitted order

An experiment reports success rates for batches at different settings.
The fitted rate should increase with the setting. Each batch is
weighted by the number of trials, so tied settings and adjacent
settings that violate the order are averaged with those weights.

```{cpp, id=isotonic-success-rate, file="figures/isotonic_success_rate.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/isotonic_regression.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    const std::vector<double> setting{1,2,2,3,4,5,6};
    const std::vector<double> rate{.20,.25,.20,.75,.50,.80,.70};
    const auto fit = stats::fit_isotonic(setting, rate,
        {.weights = {5,12,5,12,8,10,10}});
    std::vector<double> step_x, step_y;
    std::println("| First setting | Last setting | Fitted rate |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto& block : fit.blocks()) {
        std::println("| {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.3f} |", block.first_x, block.last_x, block.level);
        if (!step_y.empty()) {
            step_x.push_back(block.first_x);
            step_y.push_back(step_y.back());
        }
        step_x.push_back(block.first_x);
        step_y.push_back(block.level);
    }
    step_x.push_back(setting.back());
    step_y.push_back(fit.predict(setting.back()));
    std::println("\nAt an intermediate setting of 4.5, the fitted rate is {:.3f}.", fit.predict(4.5));

    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    graphics::RPlot::create()
        .line(step_x, step_y, "Monotone fit", {29, 78, 216}, 2.5)
        .points(setting, rate, "Batch rates", {180, 83, 9}, 5)
        .title("Weighted isotonic fit").x_label("Setting").y_label("Success rate")
        .y_limits(0, 1).size(740, 420).save_svg("build/isotonic_success_rate.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| First setting | Last setting | Fitted rate |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 1 | 0.200 |
| 2 | 2 | 0.235 |
| 3 | 4 | 0.650 |
| 5 | 6 | 0.750 |

At an intermediate setting of 4.5, the fitted rate is 0.650.
\image(figures/isotonic_success_rate.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The weighted fit pools the two batches at setting 2 and the adjacent settings 3–4 and 5–6.)
\alttext(Seven observed rates surround four increasing fitted plateaus. The plateau between settings 3 and 5 is at 0.65.)

The step stays constant until the first observed setting of the next
pooled block. Beyond the observed range, the fit holds the nearest
endpoint level.

[Ayer et al., An Empirical Distribution Function for Sampling with Incomplete Information|https://doi.org/10.1214/aoms/1177728423]
